# The := slice, Codex's review of 303, item 1: an exact identifier (the grammar, sections 3.4.2-3.4.3: `a` and a are one
# name) names the formal of a named argument as the bare spelling does -- tst(`a` := 5) and tst(`a`:=5) are the named
# supply of a (35, no call): the binder compares the payload of the name (lm_compiler_payload_same), the unit's identifier
# table interns the payload too (lm_compiler_unit_id: one code for `a` and a, as the kernel binder compares codes), and P0
# makes the Frame `:=` whatever the head's spelling.  Entry 7; 81-82 the failing step.
fn: tst (int: c 3; int: a) int
    return: c * 10 + a
end: tst
int: code 7
if: tst(`a` := 5) != 35
    code := 81
---
if: code = 7 && tst(`a`:=5) != 35
    code := 82
---
send: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
